Bima's iPhone starts with nothing. In this tutorial, Bima copies the project
identity and the two sites from Heru's iPad by scanning QR codes, so both
devices hold the same records with the same UUIDs.

## 1. Bima: create the project from Heru's QR

{% steps %}

1. On the iPad, Heru opens the `Dashboard` and taps the QR code in
   `Project overview`.
2. On the iPhone, Bima selects `Create project`, then `Scan project QR`, and
   points the camera at the iPad.
3. Bima checks that `Project UUID` matches the iPad and selects
   `Create project`.

{% /steps %}

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-project-qr.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Project QR code dialog on an iPad with the caption: scan this code when creating a new project to transfer project information"
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad"
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-create-project.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Create project screen on an iPhone with Create new project, Import project-info JSON, and Scan project QR"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

The review screen lists what the QR carries: the project UUID, name,
description, time zone, and dates. It also states what it does not carry:
records, personnel, taxa, and device settings. Those come next.

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-review-project.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Review imported project on an iPhone with project UUID 3f6c2a8e-9b41-4d7e-a5c2-71e0d4b8c913, name Tujuh teamwork 2014, time zone Asia/Jakarta, and dates 12 to 18 September 2014"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

## 2. Copy the site catalog by QR

{% steps %}

1. On the iPad, Heru opens `Sites`, shows `TJHH-L1`, and selects
   `Show QR` from the three-dot menu.
2. On the iPhone, Bima opens `Sites` and selects `Scan QR` from the three-dot
   menu.
3. In `Import site`, Bima keeps `Create new site` and selects `Continue`.
4. Repeat for `TJHH-P1`.

{% /steps %}

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-site-menu.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Sites menu on an iPhone with Show QR and Export site unavailable, and Scan QR and Import site available"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

{% aside type="caution" title="Create a new site, not an update" %}
When a site is already open on the receiving device, `Import site` selects
`Update an existing site` by default, which would overwrite that site with the
scanned one. For the second site, select `Create new site` before continuing.
{% /aside %}

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-site-import.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Import site dialog on an iPhone for TJHH-P1 with one coordinate and one person, reusing the saved Pondok Merah locality, and Create new site selected instead of updating TJHH-L1"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

Each site QR carries its geography, habitat, coordinates, and site leader. The
second site reuses the Pondok Merah locality saved by the first, so the iPhone
ends up with one locality and two sites—the same as the iPad. Heru's personnel
record arrives with the site leader.

